 { In this game, Black plays a provocative (and dubious!) opening, allowing White to take full control of the centre. From here, they use this central advantage as a springboard to launch a crushing attack on the kingside. } 1. { -KEY- } e4 b6 $6 2. d4 Bb7 3. Bd3 e6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Nbd2 { After just a handful of moves, White is already clearly better as they have built up what is known as the 'ideal centre' with pawns standing adjacent on e4 and d4. } d5 { Contesting the centre, but this plays into White's hands. } 6. e5 $1 { Grabbing space, and shutting down the activity of Black's light-squared bishop. } Ne4 $2 { For now Black's knight looks strong, but sooner or later White will force it to be exchanged for their d2 knight. } 7. O-O c5 8. c3 $1 { Keeping the central pawn chain intact. } cxd4 ( 8... f6 $2 { Pawn breaks are a typical method for undermining the centre, but here it fails after } 9. exf6 $1 gxf6 10. Re1 f5 11. Bb5 { White is already winning as Black's position is riddled with weaknesses and their king is at risk of being hunted. } ) 
9. cxd4 Be7 10. Re1 $1 { The pressure against the knight is now so great that Black is forced to initiate the exchange on d2. } Nxd2 11. Bxd2 Nc6 12. a3 { White moves to control the b4 square, avoiding any possibilities of ...Nb4 in the future. } O-O { Sooner or later Black has to castle kingside, since queenside would be too dangerous given the open c-file. } 13. Rc1 Rc8 14. Bb1 $1 { With the centre under firm control, White now begins operations against the kingside. Rook lifts with Rc1-c3 or establishing a queen-bishop battery with Qd1-c2 are now on the cards. This is a huge problem for the side that is dominated in the centre, as the combination of greater piece mobility and control of key squares (e.g. d6, f6) heavily limits the options of the defending side. } Na5 { Trying to drum up some counterplay on the queenside. } 15. Rxc8 Bxc8 { It's best for Black to keep their queen eyeing the g5 square, but the drawback to this is that Black is not covering the c2 square. } ( 15... Qxc8 $6 { If Black had captured with the queen, then the g5 square would be available to White's knight. } 16. Ng5 $1 h6 { instead, ...Bxg5 would give White a free hand on the dark squares, which could be used to propel the attack forward. } 17. Nh7 $1 { Not the only move, but already White spots the kill in sight. } Rd8 18. Qg4 { Threatening Bxh6. } Kh8 19. Qh5 $18 { Black can already resign, since there is no way to defend the f7 pawn without losing material. We can see that Black's pieces, lacking space and central control, were unable to either find activity and counter-chances on any part of the board as well as being too poorly coordinated and immobile to defend against the attack. } ) 
16. Qc2 $1 g6 { Provoking the first structural weakness. This pawn is not one that Black wanted to move, since now all of the kingside pawns are on light squares, enabling White to play a 'color complex' strategy, as discussed in the 'Weak Squares' chapter. } 17. Bh6 $1 Re8 18. Qc1 { Quickly rearranging the pieces onto dark squares. } Bd7 19. Qf4 Qc7 20. h4 { Lacking counterplay, we can already say that Black is hopelessly lost as White prepares the h4-h5 pawn break. } Rc8 21. h5 Nc4 22. hxg6 hxg6 23. Qg4 Be8 { Defending against the threat of Bxg6. } ( 23... Nxb2 $2 { Black does not have time to win a pawn, since } 24. Bxg6 $1 { leads to forced mate in a handful of moves. } ) 
24. Ng5 Nxb2 25. Nxf7 $1 { White finds a different piece sacrifice as a means of breaking through. } Bxf7 26. Bxg6 Bf8 27. Bf5+ Kh8 28. Bxf8 Rxf8 29. Qh4+ { Black resigned in view of the threat of Qh7# on the next move. In this game we saw how Black was facing an uphill struggle once White established the powerful pawn formation with pawns on d4/e5. While it's true that this structure is allowed in some openings (most notably the French Defense) there Black quickly seeks to undermine White's advantage via the ...c5 and ...f6 pawn breaks. Here, this was not possible and so Black's position quickly degraded. } 
