 { We reach an opening position in the game Short,N - Astaneh Lopez,A (2020) where my Grandmaster opponent had gone for a London System. While Black is objectively fine, I needed to be a little bit careful because although the structure is symmetrical, White's minor pieces are dominating the central squares. Therefore, White enjoys the greater central control. } 8... { -KEY- } c5 { The pawn break made sense to me, challenging White's centre while freeing up the c6 square for my knight. } 9. Qf3 $1 { Anticipating that the central control will lead to attacking opportunities, White brings the queen out and makes room for queenside castling in the future. } Nc6 10. O-O-O Bd6 $2 { Here is where things start to go wrong! At first glance the move seems really natural, but the problem is that I don't have an immediate threat here. And so my opponent already starts to move his attention to attacking on the kingside. } 11. h4 $1 Qc7 ( { The problem with exchanging White's invading knight with } 11... Bxe5 $2 { is that after } 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. Qg3 $16 { White would have been left with the bishop pair as well as serious pressure against my vulnerable dark squares. } ) 
12. Bb5 { A creative idea! White uses tactics to pin the Black knight on c6, and in this way will establish even greater control over the key central e5 square. } O-O $2 { Already you could say that this is the losing mistake, since from here the position becomes close to unplayable. In the next position we'll pick it up from here and show the right way for Black to have played in order to avoid disaster. } ( 12... axb5 $2 { is not possible because of } 13. Nxb5 Qd8 14. Nxc6 $18 { when White recovers their piece with interest. } ) 
 ( 12... cxd4 $1 { This was the best move, and is covered in the next game. } ) 
13. Bxc6 $1 bxc6 14. h5 $1 { With Black's c6 knight gone from the board, White has stabilised the situation in the centre and continues with the attack. His idea is to play h5-h6 next challenging Black's only dark-squared pawn on that side of the board. As with the previous examples, we see the link between central control and weak squares, color complexes, outposts, etc. } Nd7 $2 { The final blunder. } ( 14... h6 $2 { was not possible due to } 15. Bxh6 $18 ) 
 ( 14... Ne8 { This move was my last chance to save the game, but after } 15. Nd3 c4 16. Nc5 $16 { White would have been able to shift their focus to the c5 outpost when it's hard to see how Black can hope to survive given that White stands better on both flanks of the board. } ) 
15. h6 $1 Bxe5 ( 15... Nxe5 { would also quickly lose after } 16. dxe5 Bxe5 17. hxg7 Kxg7 18. Qg3 Kh8 19. Rxh7 Kxh7 20. Rh1 ) 
16. hxg7 $1 { A clever in-between move. Now the king's pawn cover is torn apart. } Bxf4 { I had no choice but to accept losing an exchange. } 17. gxf8=Q+ Nxf8 18. Qxf4 $18 { The dust has settled and White is left with a winning material advantage. I struggled on for a handful of moves before resigning. My strong opponent made light work of my king once he had established complete control of the centre. Let's now revisit this game in our next example and take a look at how I could have better handled the problems in the centre. } 
