 ( { We mentioned in the first part of our coverage of the game Short, N - Astaneh Lopez, A (2020) that here my move } 12. Qxf4 O-O $2 { marked the beginning of the end. The problem is that after this there was almost no way to stop the train from rolling on the kingside, due to White's iron grip in the centre. Now, we'll take a look at the way in which I should have gone about business instead. } ) 
 { -KEY- } cxd4 $1 { The first of several steps to undermine White's structural control. } 13. Bxc6+ { Now it is necessary to exchange on c6. } ( 13. exd4 $2 { Maintaining the tension on the b5 square no longer works, because of the hidden effect of the c1-h6 diagonal opening up. } axb5 $1 14. Nxb5 Qb8 15. Nxc6 $4 { With a pawn on e3, this move would be crushing. However, now Black has a winning intermezzo (an in-between move). } ( 15. Nxd6 { would keep White in the game. However, after a complicated sequence Black would be left with the better chances: } Qxd6 16. Nxf7 Qf8 17. Nxh8 Rxa2 { White's knight is trapped, so Black instead uses his move to grab the pawn on a2. } 18. Kb1 Ra4 19. Be5 Qxh8 $17 { Black's two minor pieces are worth a little more than a rook, though White continues to keep practical chances. } ) 
Bxf4 16. Kb1 bxc6 $19 { Black emerges two pieces to the good. } ) 
bxc6 { But now we see that the exchange has left Black with a new pawn on the c-file, which turns out to be very important. } 14. exd4 c5 $1 { And this is the reason why! White is out of ammunition for supporting the d4 pawn, and so had I played this way my opponent would not have been able to maintain the level of central stability that allowed him to launch a devastating attack. } 15. Rhe1 ( { White's rook now has to support the knight, since Black was threatening }  cxd4 { when after } 16. Rxd4 Bxe5 { would win a piece. } ) 
cxd4 16. Rxd4 O-O { Now castling is safe. } 17. g4 { White might have tried to continue the attack by pushing the g-pawn, but here we can see that Black's newly improved central control would have offered him additional defensive resources: } ( 17. h5 $6 { would no longer be convincing, since after } h6 $15 { White does not have the possibility of } 18. Bxh6 { as in the game, because Black would reply } Bxe5 { winning a piece. } ) 
Bb7 $1 18. g5 Ne4 $13 { Now all Black's pieces are developed and centralised, with constant pressure against White's e5 knight. If White is not careful he will be the one who finds himself on the defensive soon, since after all, Black has semi open b- and c-files for his own attack. In short (pun may or may not be intentional), I could have avoided my opponent's powerful attack had I been sufficiently alert and agile in undermining his grip on the central squares. Instead, I recklessly castled into the fire and from there my Grandmaster opponent barely had to break a sweat in order to push his attack through. When facing the possibility of an attack, it is often essential to undermine the opponent's centre as quickly as possible. This concludes the part on attacking ideas. We'll now finish the section by showing a couple of techniques that are also useful to consider when you have the centre, though we'll only briefly touch upon them since they are points of discussion elsewhere in the course. } 
