 { This type of play has already been introduced in the preceding chapter on 'Space'. There, we looked at a few examples of one side 'steamrolling' the opponent. Since the two topics are closely linked, it's no surprise to find the same possibility when you have greater central control. The following position is taken from an online game where Magnus Carlsen had the White pieces against Turkish GM Vahap Sanal. Can you find the strong move that he played here, and the idea associated with it? } 1. { -KEY- } Bxc5 $1 { By giving up the bishop, White forces Black's d-pawn to move to the c-file and paves the path for the advance of his central pawns. } ( 1. f4 { would be less convincing, since now after } Ned7 { the knight on d7 defends its colleague on c5, although even here White could go for a nice sacrificial idea after the moves } 2. e5 $1 dxe5 3. f5 { White breaks on the kingside, and Black has no good way of defending the g6 pawn. Note how by sacrificing a pawn on e5, White heavily disrupts the activity of the enemy pieces. We'll revisit this topic in our next example. } ) 
dxc5 2. f4 $1 { Correct! White builds up a deadly pawn centre. } Ng4 ( 2... Nd7 { The retreating move would not have changed the evaluation. } 3. e5 $1 Nf8 4. Bf3 $18 { Black is unable to combat this pawn centre, and White can go for a slow build-up on the d-file before breaking through. Besides this, White can also target the vulnerable c5 pawn whenever they wish. } ) 
3. Bxg4 hxg4 4. e5 { The advanced centre cannot be contested, since Black has no convincing pawn breaks. Add to this the weak pawns on g4 and c5 and we can say that the game is already over. } cxd5 5. cxd5 { Recapturing with the pawn, ensuring to keep the maximum advantage in the centre. } Qd7 6. a4 b6 7. h5 $1 { White's central dominance gives him license to attack on the kingside. } Qf5 ( 7... gxh5 { is hopeless, since after } 8. Rh1 { White recovers his pawn on the next move, when the open h-file makes it impossible to defend the king. } ) 
8. hxg6 $1 fxg6 ( { If } 8... Qxc2 9. Rxc2 fxg6 { White has many ways to win, with perhaps the cleanest being to play } 10. Rh1 $18 { with the intention of picking up the g4 pawn next. } ) 
9. d6 { There were many ways to win, but Magnus finally decides to open up the position when Black will be unable to defend their numerous weaknesses. } exd6 10. Rxd6 Rd8 11. Nb5 { Making use of the queenside outpost. } ( 11. Rxg6 { was even stronger. } ) 
Rxd6 12. Nxd6 { And now of the central outpost! } Qe6 13. Qc4 Qxc4 14. Nxc4 $18 { The knight on c4 is a monster, playing both an offensive and defensive role. White soon converted the winning endgame. } 
