 { A concept that we'll run into on a few occasions within this course is what I call 'convergence zones'. This is a square through which several pieces intersect, making that square especially important. The technique we'll now take a look at involves making a positional pawn sacrifice in order to have an effect on a convergence zone (or zones). Let's show what we mean by way of an example, taken from the game Botvinnik,M - Pomar Salamanca,A (1962). } 12. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { White spotted that the e5 square is a convergence zone for the Black pieces, while the e4 square is a convergence zone for the White pieces. With this pawn sacrifice, he gains access to e4 while simultaneously shutting down the e5 square for Black's army. } fxe5 13. f5 $1 { This is the key follow-up. Black's bishop-queen battery is left paralysed. } Bf7 14. Ne4 $16 { White, on the other hand, is now the one to benefit from the e4 square which becomes a powerful central outpost for his knight. } ( 14. Qg4 $18 { was even stronger, since here } O-O-O { would not have been possible on account of } 15. f6 { , winning a piece. } ) 
O-O-O 15. Qg4 { Threatening 16.f6+. } Kb8 16. Qxg7 $18 { White had recovered their pawn with a near-winning position. Botvinnik went on to win the game, but we'll stop here as the position got chaotic and the character of the struggle became largely dynamic. It's also interesting to note that in the final position Black has the more advanced pawn chain in the centre, but White is the one that has all of the central dominance. This reminds us of a concept discussed earlier, namely that the effectiveness of your pieces is a more important factor than the number of squares you control. This concludes our section on the power of the centre, where our main focus was on the connection between central control and attacking. In the final two examples we briefly touched on a couple of other concepts that we spend more time on elsewhere in the course. From here, we'll be moving on to our second section where we'll flip the script and look at ways of fighting against the centre! See you there. } 
 1-0
